return [
    1 => [
        'title' => 'Trauma Exposure (Criterion A) & The Stress-Response Continuum',
        'slug' => 'trauma-exposure-criterion-a-stress-continuum',
        'description' => 'DSM-5 Criterion A operational definition, four qualifying pathways, strict media exposure exclusion, and the stress continuum.',
        'overview' => "Trauma- and Stressor-Related Disorders represent a distinct diagnostic category in DSM-5 defined by an explicit etiological requirement: exposure to a traumatic or stressful event is listed explicitly as a required diagnostic criterion. In DSM-IV, PTSD was categorized under Anxiety Disorders; however, DSM-5 separated trauma disorders into their own dedicated chapter, recognizing that posttraumatic pathology encompasses not only fear and anxiety, but profound disruptions in mood, guilt, shame, anhedonia, dissociation, and aggressive arousal.\n\nA fundamental diagnostic anchor in this chapter is Criterion A trauma exposure. In DSM-5, the definition of what constitutes a qualifying trauma was tightened significantly. A qualifying traumatic event requires exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence. The subjective requirement from DSM-IV—that the person's initial response involved 'intense fear, helplessness, or horror' (Criterion A2)—was completely eliminated in DSM-5, recognizing that acute trauma responses vary widely and may manifest as emotional numbness, dissociation, or tactical action rather than overt terror.\n\nFurthermore, DSM-5 established strict parameters regarding indirect exposure: hearing about a trauma qualifies ONLY if the event involved a close family member or close friend and was violent or accidental. Crucially, exposure through electronic media, television, movies, or pictures does NOT qualify as Criterion A trauma unless this exposure is directly work-related (e.g., police officers viewing child exploitation imagery).",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence in one (or more) of the following four pathways:\n\n1. Direct Exposure: Directly experiencing the traumatic event (e.g., surviving combat, severe motor vehicle accident, physical assault, rape).\n2. Witnessing in Person: Witnessing, in person, the event as it occurred to others (e.g., witnessing a homicide or catastrophic explosion; does not include hearing about it after the fact).\n3. Indirect Learning: Learning that the traumatic event occurred to a close family member or close friend. In cases of actual or threatened death of a family member or friend, the event must have been violent or accidental (e.g., sudden death from chronic illness does NOT qualify).\n4. Repeated/Extreme Work-Related Exposure: Experiencing repeated or extreme exposure to aversive details of the traumatic event (e.g., first responders collecting human remains; police officers repeatedly exposed to details of child abuse).\n\nCRITICAL EXCLUSION: Criterion A explicitly states: 'This does not apply to exposure through electronic media, television, movies, or pictures, unless this exposure is work-related.'",
        'specifiers' => "Trauma evaluations document the specific mechanism of Criterion A exposure and note whether multiple or chronic cumulative traumas are present (complex trauma).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Criterion A Trauma vs. Non-Traumatic Psychosocial Stressors: Divorce, bankruptcy, job loss, infidelity, and romantic breakups are distressing life events, but they do NOT qualify as Criterion A trauma because they do not involve actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence. Reactions to these stressors are classified under Adjustment Disorders, NOT PTSD.\n\nDirect Witnessing vs. Media Viewing: Watching continuous news broadcasts of a terrorist attack or school shooting does NOT qualify as Criterion A trauma under DSM-5 guidelines.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 35-year-old paramedic who presents with nightmares and flashbacks after responding to a mass-casualty highway collision where they physically triaged deceased and mutilated victims.\n\nEvaluation verifies qualifying Criterion A exposure via Pathway 4 (repeated extreme occupational exposure to aversive details of actual death and serious injury). Conversely, an office worker who watched the identical collision unfold on social media news feeds does NOT meet Criterion A, illustrating the strict exclusion of non-occupational electronic media exposure.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Criterion A requires exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence via 4 pathways: (1) direct experience, (2) witnessing in person, (3) learning of violent/accidental trauma to close family/friend, or (4) repeated work-related exposure to aversive details. Media, TV, or video exposure does NOT qualify unless work-related. DSM-5 eliminated the A2 requirement of 'fear, helplessness, or horror'. Ordinary life stressors (divorce, job loss) are evaluated under Adjustment Disorders.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Four Criterion A Pathways', 'icon' => '🚪', 'definition' => 'Direct experience, in-person witnessing, learning of violent trauma to loved one, or repeated occupational exposure.'],
