    2 => [
        'title' => 'Posttraumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD: Intrusion, Avoidance, Cognition/Mood, Arousal)',
        'slug' => 'ptsd-diagnostic-criteria-four-clusters',
        'description' => 'The 4 symptom clusters (Intrusion, Avoidance, Negative alterations in cognition/mood, Arousal/reactivity), 1-month duration, and the dissociative specifier.',
        'overview' => "Posttraumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD) is a severe, debilitating trauma-related condition characterized by the failure of normal post-event recovery, leading to enduring alterations in memory processing, threat appraisal, emotional regulation, and neuroendocrine functioning. In DSM-5, the diagnostic criteria for PTSD were fundamentally overhauled, expanding from the three symptom clusters of DSM-IV into four distinct, empirically validated symptom clusters: Intrusion, Persistent Avoidance, Negative Alterations in Cognitions and Mood, and Alterations in Arousal and Reactivity.\n\nThis four-cluster model reflects the clinical reality that PTSD is far more than an anxiety disorder. The addition of the 'Negative Alterations in Cognitions and Mood' cluster captures pervasive cognitive distortions, trauma-related amnesia, chronic negative affective states (guilt, shame, horror), persistent blame of self or others, and profound feelings of alienation from others.\n\nA major diagnostic requirement in PTSD is duration: the full constellation of symptoms across all four clusters must persist for more than one month following trauma exposure. Symptoms lasting less than one month are evaluated under Acute Stress Disorder. Furthermore, DSM-5 introduced a dedicated Dissociative Subtype of PTSD, identifying individuals who experience prominent depersonalization or derealization in response to trauma reminders.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: Qualifying trauma exposure across 1 of 4 pathways.\n\nCriterion B (Intrusion Symptoms - requires 1 or more):\n1. Recurrent, involuntary, and intrusive distressing memories of the event.\n2. Recurrent distressing dreams related to the event.\n3. Dissociative reactions (e.g., flashbacks) in which the individual feels or acts as if the event were recurring.\n4. Intense or prolonged psychological distress at exposure to internal or external cues resembling the event.\n5. Marked physiological reactions to internal or external cues resembling the event.\n\nCriterion C (Persistent Avoidance - requires 1 or more):\n1. Avoidance of or efforts to avoid distressing memories, thoughts, or feelings about the event.\n2. Avoidance of or efforts to avoid external reminders (people, places, conversations, activities, objects, situations) that arouse distressing memories.\n\nCriterion D (Negative Alterations in Cognitions and Mood - requires 2 or more):\n1. Inability to remember an important aspect of the event (dissociative amnesia, not due to head injury or alcohol).\n2. Persistent and exaggerated negative beliefs or expectations about oneself, others, or the world ('I am bad', 'No one can be trusted').\n3. Persistent, distorted cognitions about the cause or consequences of the event that lead the individual to blame himself/herself or others.\n4. Persistent negative emotional state (fear, horror, anger, guilt, or shame).\n5. Markedly diminished interest or participation in significant activities.\n6. Feelings of detachment or estrangement from others.\n7. Persistent inability to experience positive emotions (happiness, satisfaction, love).\n\nCriterion E (Alterations in Arousal and Reactivity - requires 2 or more):\n1. Irritable behavior and angry outbursts (with little or no provocation), typically expressed as verbal or physical aggression.\n2. Reckless or self-destructive behavior.\n3. Hypervigilance.\n4. Exaggerated startle response.\n5. Problems with concentration.\n6. Sleep disturbance.\n\nCriterion F (Duration): Symptoms in Criteria B, C, D, and E must persist for MORE than 1 month.\nCriterion G requires clinically significant distress or functional impairment. Criterion H rules out substances and medical conditions.",
        'specifiers' => "Specifier: 'With dissociative symptoms' (assigned if the individual meets full PTSD criteria and experiences persistent or recurrent symptoms of either Depersonalization [feeling detached from one's mind/body] or Derealization [unreality of surroundings]).\n\nSpecifier: 'With delayed expression' (assigned if the full diagnostic criteria are not met until at least 6 months after the traumatic event, although some symptoms may have begun earlier).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "PTSD vs. Acute Stress Disorder: Differentiated strictly by duration: Acute Stress Disorder lasts between 3 days and 1 month following trauma; PTSD requires duration of MORE than 1 month.\n\nPTSD vs. Major Depressive Disorder: MDD shares negative mood, anhedonia, and sleep disturbance, but LACKS Criterion B intrusion symptoms (flashbacks, traumatic nightmares) and Criterion C avoidance of trauma reminders.\n\nPTSD vs. Borderline Personality Disorder: While both involve emotional dysregulation and trauma histories, PTSD is characterized by re-experiencing flashbacks and physiological reactivity to specific traumatic reminders.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 28-year-old combat veteran evaluated six months after returning from deployment. The veteran experiences vivid flashbacks and nightmares of an explosion (Intrusion: 2 symptoms), refuses to drive on highways or attend family gatherings (Avoidance: 2 symptoms), feels completely detached from their spouse and believes the entire world is corrupt (Cognition/Mood: 2 symptoms), and exhibits extreme irritability, insomnia, and an exaggerated startle response to loud fireworks (Arousal: 3 symptoms).\n\nWhen exposed to helicopter sounds, the veteran feels as though their body is floating outside their physical form (depersonalization). Symptoms have persisted for six months. Meeting criteria across all four clusters for >1 month with depersonalization, the clinical formulation confirms Posttraumatic Stress Disorder, With Dissociative Symptoms, With Delayed Expression.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: PTSD requires all 4 clusters: Intrusion (>=1), Avoidance (>=1), Negative Cognitions/Mood (>=2), and Arousal/Reactivity (>=2). Duration MUST be > 1 month (symptoms lasting 3 days to 1 month are Acute Stress Disorder). Persistent avoidance (Criterion C) is a mandatory cluster. The Dissociative Subtype includes depersonalization or derealization. Delayed expression is coded if full criteria are not met until >= 6 months after the event.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The Four-Cluster Architecture', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Intrusion (1+), Avoidance (1+), Negative Cognitions/Mood (2+), and Arousal/Reactivity (2+).'],
            ['title' => 'The > 1 Month Duration Floor', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Symptoms must endure for more than 30 days; symptoms resolving prior to 1 month represent Acute Stress Disorder.'],
            ['title' => 'Dissociative Subtype Specifier', 'icon' => '🌫️', 'definition' => 'Identifies individuals experiencing prominent depersonalization or derealization in response to trauma cues.'],
            ['title' => 'Delayed Expression Specifier', 'icon' => '⏳', 'definition' => 'Assigned when full diagnostic criteria are not satisfied until at least 6 months after the index traumatic event.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Flashback', 'definition' => 'A dissociative state wherein an individual feels or acts as if a past traumatic event were recurring in the present moment, losing contact with current reality.'],
            ['term' => 'Depersonalization', 'definition' => 'Persistent or recurrent experiences of feeling detached from, and as if one were an outside observer of, one\'s mental processes or body.'],
            ['term' => 'Derealization', 'definition' => 'Persistent or recurrent experiences of unreality of surroundings, perceiving the world as dreamlike, distant, or distorted.'],
            ['term' => 'Hypervigilance', 'definition' => 'A state of enhanced sensory sensitivity and constant scanning of the environment for potential threats and danger.']
        ]
    ],
    3 => [
        'title' => 'PTSD in Children 6 Years and Younger: Developmental Criteria',
        'slug' => 'ptsd-children-6-years-younger-developmental',
        'description' => 'Developmentally sensitive criteria, traumatic play, dream content without recognizable themes, and lower symptom thresholds.',
        'overview' => "Recognizing that young children lack the advanced verbal articulation, abstract cognitive reasoning, and introspective capabilities required to report classic adult PTSD symptoms, DSM-5 introduced a dedicated developmental subtype: Posttraumatic Stress Disorder in Children 6 Years and Younger. In DSM-IV, young traumatized children were severely underdiagnosed because criteria demanded complex internal verbal self-reports (such as reporting that dreams were specifically about the trauma or articulating persistent negative beliefs about the self).\n\nThe preschool developmental criteria adapt symptom expressions to age-appropriate behavioral manifestations. For example, intrusive traumatic memories in young children rarely manifest as verbal recollections; instead, they appear as repetitive traumatic play wherein the child compulsively reenacts themes or aspects of the trauma using toys or peer interactions. Distressing dreams may occur without recognizable traumatic content, manifesting simply as terrifying nightmares followed by night terrors and bedwetting.\n\nFurthermore, DSM-5 significantly lowered the diagnostic symptom thresholds for children under age 7. Because young children cannot readily articulate cognitive appraisals, the adult Avoidance and Negative Cognitions/Mood clusters were merged into a single combined cluster requiring only one symptom, ensuring appropriate diagnostic sensitivity.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: In children 6 years and younger, exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence by: (1) directly experiencing the event; (2) witnessing in person (especially caregivers); or (3) learning that the event occurred to a parent or caregiver.\n\nCriterion B (Intrusion Symptoms - requires 1 or more):\n1. Recurrent, involuntary, and intrusive distressing memories of the traumatic event (may not appear distressing and may be expressed as play reenactment).\n2. Recurrent distressing dreams (content may not be recognizable).\n3. Dissociative reactions (e.g., flashbacks) or trauma-specific reenactments in play.\n4. Significant psychological distress at trauma reminders.\n5. Marked physiological reactions to reminders.\n\nCriterion C (Combined Avoidance OR Negative Alterations in Cognition - requires 1 or more):\n1. Persistent avoidance of activities, places, or physical reminders.\n2. Persistent avoidance of people, conversations, or interpersonal reminders.\n3. Substantially increased frequency of negative emotional states (fear, guilt, sadness, shame).\n4. Markedly diminished interest or participation in significant activities (including constriction of play).\n5. Socially withdrawn behavior.\n6. Persistent reduction in positive emotions.\n\nCriterion D (Alterations in Arousal - requires 2 or more):\n1. Irritable behavior and angry outbursts (extreme temper tantrums) with little provocation.\n2. Hypervigilance.\n3. Exaggerated startle response.\n4. Problems with concentration.\n5. Sleep disturbance.\n\nCriterion E (Duration): Symptoms in Criteria B, C, and D must persist for MORE than 1 month.",
        'specifiers' => "Specifier: 'With dissociative symptoms' (Depersonalization or Derealization; in young children, may manifest as freezing, staring blankly, or unresponsiveness during trauma reminders).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "PTSD in Young Children vs. Reactive Attachment Disorder (RAD): In RAD, the child exhibits inhibited, emotionally withdrawn behavior toward adult caregivers due to pathogenic social neglect, but lacks traumatic intrusive play and physiological startle responses.\n\nPTSD in Young Children vs. ADHD: Hyperarousal in traumatized children (restlessness, concentration difficulties) can mimic ADHD; however, PTSD features traumatic play, nightmares, and constriction of play that are absent in ADHD.\n\nPTSD vs. Oppositional Defiant Disorder (ODD): Traumatized children frequently exhibit explosive temper tantrums secondary to hyperarousal; careful trauma history reveals underlying intrusion symptoms.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 4-year-old child who survived a severe multi-car collision with their mother three months ago. Following the event, the child repeatedly smashes toy ambulances together for hours, screaming 'Crash! Crash!' (traumatic play reenactment).\n\nThe child has nightmares waking in terror without explaining the dream, refuses to enter the family car, exhibits severe constriction of creative play, and throws extreme screaming tantrums whenever loud traffic noises are heard (hyperarousal, temper tantrums). The clinical formulation confirms Posttraumatic Stress Disorder in Children 6 Years and Younger.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: DSM-5 established separate developmental criteria for PTSD in children aged 6 years and younger. Intrusive memories manifest as repetitive play reenactment; distressing dreams may lack recognizable traumatic content. Avoidance and Negative Cognitions are combined into a single cluster requiring only ONE symptom. Temper tantrums reflect trauma-induced hyperarousal. Duration must be > 1 month.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Developmental Play Reenactment', 'icon' => '🧸', 'definition' => 'Trauma intrusion in young children manifests behaviorally through compulsive, repetitive play recreating trauma themes.'],
            ['title' => 'Content-Free Traumatic Nightmares', 'icon' => '🌙', 'definition' => 'Distressing dreams in preschoolers frequently feature generalized terror without clear narrative memory of the trauma.'],
            ['title' => 'Combined Avoidance/Cognition Cluster', 'icon' => '🧩', 'definition' => 'Avoidance and cognitive changes are merged into a single cluster requiring only 1 symptom due to limited verbal expression.'],
            ['title' => 'Lowered Diagnostic Threshold', 'icon' => '📉', 'definition' => 'Accommodates developmental limitations by reducing total symptom counts required for diagnosis in young children.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Traumatic Play', 'definition' => 'Compulsive, repetitive play in young children that reenacts aspects or themes of a traumatic event without providing emotional relief.'],
            ['term' => 'Constriction of Play', 'definition' => 'A marked developmental reduction in exploratory, imaginative, or social play behaviors in traumatized young children.'],
            ['term' => 'Developmental Regression', 'definition' => 'The loss of previously acquired developmental milestones (e.g., bedwetting, baby talk) following trauma exposure.'],
            ['term' => 'Child-Parent Psychotherapy (CPP)', 'definition' => 'An evidence-based trauma-informed treatment for children aged 0-5 and their primary caregivers focused on restoring attachment security.']
        ]
    ],
    4 => [
        'title' => 'Acute Stress Disorder (ASD: 3 Days to 1 Month Duration)',
        'slug' => 'acute-stress-disorder-duration-criteria',
        'description' => 'The 3-day to 1-month duration window, 9 of 14 symptom menu across 5 categories, and progression to PTSD.',
        'overview' => "Acute Stress Disorder (ASD) represents an acute psychological reaction occurring immediately following exposure to a qualifying Criterion A traumatic event. Introduced in DSM-IV to identify traumatized individuals who might subsequently develop chronic PTSD, ASD serves as a vital clinical classification for acute trauma intervention in emergency departments, disaster relief settings, and crisis counseling.\n\nThe defining hallmark of Acute Stress Disorder is its strict temporal boundaries: symptoms must develop within days of trauma exposure and persist for at least 3 days up to a maximum duration of 1 month. If symptoms resolve within 30 days, the diagnosis remains Acute Stress Disorder; if symptoms persist beyond one month, the diagnosis must be immediately updated to Posttraumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD).\n\nA major conceptual change in DSM-5 was the restructuring of the symptom criteria. In DSM-IV, ASD required dissociative symptoms (amnesia, depersonalization, derealization). DSM-5 eliminated the mandatory dissociation requirement, adopting a flexible '9 of 14' symptom structure drawn across five categories: intrusion, negative mood, dissociation, avoidance, and arousal. This revision recognizes that acute trauma presents with diverse emotional phenotypes.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: Exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence across 1 of 4 pathways.\n\nCriterion B: Presence of nine (or more) of the following 14 symptoms from any of the five categories (intrusion, negative mood, dissociation, avoidance, arousal), beginning or worsening after the traumatic event occurred:\n\nIntrusion Symptoms:\n1. Recurrent, involuntary, and intrusive distressing memories of the traumatic event.\n2. Recurrent distressing dreams related to the event.\n3. Dissociative reactions (e.g., flashbacks).\n4. Intense psychological or physiological distress in response to trauma cues.\n\nNegative Mood:\n5. Persistent inability to experience positive emotions (happiness, satisfaction, love).\n\nDissociative Symptoms:\n6. An altered sense of the reality of one's surroundings or oneself (e.g., seeing oneself from others' perspective, being in a daze, time slowing).\n7. Inability to remember an important aspect of the event (dissociative amnesia).\n\nAvoidance Symptoms:\n8. Efforts to avoid distressing memories, thoughts, or feelings about the event.\n9. Efforts to avoid external reminders (people, places, conversations, objects) that arouse memories.\n\nArousal Symptoms:\n10. Sleep disturbance.\n11. Irritable behavior and angry outbursts (with little or no provocation).\n12. Hypervigilance.\n13. Problems with concentration.\n14. Exaggerated startle response.\n\nCriterion C (Duration): Duration of the disturbance is 3 days to 1 month after trauma exposure. (Note: Symptoms typically begin immediately, but persistence for at least 3 days is required).\n\nCriterion D requires clinically significant distress or functional impairment. Criterion E rules out substances and medical conditions.",
        'specifiers' => "Acute Stress Disorder does not have separate subtype specifiers, but clinicians record whether the presentation features prominent dissociation and monitor the patient for transition to PTSD.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Acute Stress Disorder vs. PTSD: Differentiated strictly by duration: ASD persists for 3 days to 1 month; PTSD persists for MORE than 1 month. If an individual with ASD remains symptomatic on day 31, the diagnosis converts to PTSD.\n\nASD vs. Normal Acute Stress Reaction: Mild transient distress lasting 1-2 days without 9 of 14 symptoms represents normal human stress responsiveness, not ASD.\n\nASD vs. Adjustment Disorder: In Adjustment Disorder, the stressor does NOT meet Criterion A (e.g., divorce, job loss); if Criterion A trauma is present and symptoms meet criteria, ASD takes precedence.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 25-year-old bank teller who survived an armed bank robbery 10 days ago during which a coworker was shot. The teller experiences vivid flashbacks, feels emotionally numb, cannot enter the bank, has panic attacks upon hearing loud noises, cannot sleep, and feels as though the world is moving in slow motion (derealization).\n\nSymptoms have persisted for 10 days. The teller meets 10 of the 14 DSM-5 criteria across intrusion, dissociation, avoidance, and arousal. Because symptoms have lasted for 10 days (falling squarely within the 3-day to 1-month window), the diagnosis is Acute Stress Disorder. If symptoms persist beyond 30 days, the diagnosis will be recoded to PTSD.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Acute Stress Disorder duration is strictly 3 days to 1 month following Criterion A trauma. Diagnosis requires at least 9 of 14 symptoms drawn from across 5 categories (intrusion, negative mood, dissociation, avoidance, arousal). DSM-5 eliminated the mandatory dissociation requirement from DSM-IV. Approximately 50% of individuals with ASD subsequently develop chronic PTSD. If symptoms exceed 1 month, recode to PTSD.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The 3-Day to 1-Month Window', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Symptoms must last at least 3 days and less than 30 days; crossing 1 month converts diagnosis to PTSD.'],
            ['title' => 'The 9 of 14 Symptom Menu', 'icon' => '🔢', 'definition' => 'Requires 9 of 14 symptoms from any of the 5 categories (intrusion, negative mood, dissociation, avoidance, arousal).'],
            ['title' => 'Elimination of Mandatory Dissociation', 'icon' => '🔄', 'definition' => 'DSM-5 removed the requirement that dissociation must be present, accommodating non-dissociative acute trauma reactions.'],
            ['title' => 'PTSD Conversion Tracking', 'icon' => '📈', 'definition' => 'Approximately half of all ASD patients progress to full PTSD, necessitating proactive trauma-focused CBT.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Acute Trauma Phase', 'definition' => 'The initial 30-day window following trauma exposure during which acute biological and psychological crisis interventions occur.'],
            ['term' => 'Dissociative Category', 'definition' => 'One of the five symptom categories in ASD, including depersonalization, derealization, and dissociative amnesia.'],
            ['term' => 'Diagnostic Conversion', 'definition' => 'The mandatory clinical updating of an Acute Stress Disorder diagnosis to PTSD when symptoms persist beyond 30 days.'],
            ['term' => 'Critical Incident Stress Debriefing (CISD)', 'definition' => 'A controversial single-session psychological debriefing historically used after trauma, now discouraged due to evidence it may impede natural recovery.']
        ]
    ],
    5 => [
        'title' => 'Adjustment Disorders: Marked Distress Out of Proportion',
        'slug' => 'adjustment-disorders-marked-distress-subtypes',
        'description' => 'The 3-month onset rule, 6-month resolution rule, marked distress out of proportion, and the 6 clinical subtypes.',
        'overview' => "Adjustment Disorders represent common clinical conditions characterized by the development of emotional or behavioral symptoms in response to an identifiable psychosocial stressor. In DSM-5, Adjustment Disorders were reclassified from their historical residual status and placed into the core Trauma- and Stressor-Related Disorders chapter. This reorganization recognizes that adjustment disorders represent the mild-to-moderate end of the stress-response continuum, bridging normal stress reactions and severe conditions like PTSD.\n\nA fundamental diagnostic requirement of Adjustment Disorders is the '3-month onset and 6-month resolution' rule: emotional or behavioral symptoms must develop within three months of the onset of an identifiable stressor (such as divorce, business failure, medical illness diagnosis, or family conflict). Crucially, once the stressor or its consequences have terminated, the symptoms must resolve within six months. If symptoms persist beyond six months after the stressor has ended, an adjustment disorder can no longer be diagnosed.\n\nFurthermore, the reaction must be clinically significant, indicated by either marked distress that is out of proportion to the severity of the stressor (accounting for cultural context) or significant functional impairment in social, occupational, or academic spheres. Critically, the disturbance must not meet the criteria for another mental disorder (such as Major Depressive Disorder).",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: The development of emotional or behavioral symptoms in response to an identifiable stressor(s) occurring within 3 months of the onset of the stressor(s).\n\nCriterion B: These symptoms or behaviors are clinically significant, as evidenced by one or both of the following:\n1. Marked distress that is out of proportion to the severity or intensity of the stressor, taking into account the external context and the cultural factors that might influence symptom severity and expression.\n2. Significant impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning.\n\nCriterion C: The stress-related disturbance does not meet the criteria for another mental disorder and is not merely an exacerbation of a preexisting mental disorder.\n\nCriterion D: The symptoms do not represent normal bereavement.\n\nCriterion E: Once the stressor or its consequences have terminated, the symptoms do not persist for more than an additional 6 months.",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians must specify one of six clinical subtypes based on the predominant symptom presentation:\n1. With depressed mood (low mood, tearfulness, or feelings of hopelessness).\n2. With anxiety (nervousness, worry, jitteriness, or separation anxiety).\n3. With mixed anxiety and depressed mood (combination of depression and anxiety).\n4. With disturbance of conduct (violating the rights of others or major age-appropriate societal norms, such as truancy, vandalism, reckless driving, or fighting).\n5. With mixed disturbance of emotions and conduct (both emotional symptoms and conduct disturbances).\n6. Unspecified (maladaptive reactions to stressors that do not fit the other subtypes).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Adjustment Disorder vs. Major Depressive Disorder: If a patient's depressive symptoms meet full 5-of-9 criteria for a Major Depressive Episode, Major Depressive Disorder is diagnosed, NOT Adjustment Disorder. Adjustment disorder is diagnosed only when symptoms fall short of full MDD.\n\nAdjustment Disorder vs. Normal Stress Reaction: Normal stress responses do not involve marked distress out of proportion to the stressor or severe functional occupational/social disability.\n\nAdjustment Disorder vs. PTSD / ASD: In PTSD and ASD, the stressor must be a qualifying Criterion A trauma (death, serious injury, sexual violence); in Adjustment Disorder, the stressor is an ordinary psychosocial event (divorce, job loss, illness).",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 42-year-old corporate executive who was unexpectedly laid off two months ago. The executive has felt anxious and down, crying occasionally, and having trouble sleeping.\n\nHowever, the executive retains a normal appetite, has no psychomotor retardation, no suicidal ideation, and can enjoy hobbies when distracted. The symptoms clearly developed within three months of the job loss, cause distress out of proportion to normal job loss, but fall short of the 5 symptoms required for a major depressive episode. The clinical formulation confirms Adjustment Disorder, With mixed anxiety and depressed mood.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Adjustment Disorder symptoms must develop within 3 months of an identifiable stressor. Symptoms must resolve within 6 months after the stressor (or its consequences) terminates. If symptoms meet full criteria for Major Depressive Disorder, MDD takes absolute precedence—Adjustment Disorder is not diagnosed. The 6 subtypes are: With depressed mood, With anxiety, With mixed anxiety/depression, With disturbance of conduct, With mixed emotions/conduct, and Unspecified. Normal bereavement is excluded.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The 3-Month Onset Rule', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Symptoms must emerge within 90 days of the occurrence of the identifiable psychosocial stressor.'],
            ['title' => 'The 6-Month Termination Rule', 'icon' => '⌛', 'definition' => 'Symptoms must resolve within 6 months once the stressor or its direct consequences have completely ended.'],
            ['title' => 'Out of Proportion Distress', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Clinical severity requires distress exceeding what is expected, accounting for cultural and environmental context.'],
            ['title' => 'Subthreshold Requirement', 'icon' => '📉', 'definition' => 'Must not satisfy full criteria for another Axis I condition (such as MDD, GAD, or PTSD).']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Psychosocial Stressor', 'definition' => 'An external life event or transition (divorce, job loss, academic failure) that triggers emotional strain without physical trauma.'],
            ['term' => 'Disturbance of Conduct', 'definition' => 'An adjustment subtype characterized by behavioral rule-breaking, truancy, vandalism, or fighting in response to stress.'],
            ['term' => 'Proportionality Test', 'definition' => 'The clinical evaluation of whether an individual\'s emotional distress exceeds expected cultural norms for a given life challenge.'],
            ['term' => 'Stressor Termination', 'definition' => 'The point at which an active stressor or its acute legal/financial consequences have definitively ceased.']
        ]
    ],
    6 => [
        'title' => 'Dissociative Identity Disorder (DID: 2+ Distinct Personality States)',
        'slug' => 'dissociative-identity-disorder-did',
        'description' => 'Two or more distinct personality states (alters), recurrent dissociative amnesia, severe childhood trauma etiology, and cultural spirit possession.',
        'overview' => "Dissociative Identity Disorder (DID)—historically termed Multiple Personality Disorder—represents the most severe, complex condition within the Dissociative Disorders spectrum. DID is characterized by a disruption of identity characterized by two or more distinct personality states (frequently described in some cultures as an experience of possession), accompanied by recurrent gaps in the recall of everyday events, important personal information, and/or traumatic events that are inconsistent with ordinary forgetting.\n\nThe etiology of DID is fundamentally anchored in severe, chronic, inescapable childhood relational trauma—most commonly prolonged physical, sexual, or emotional abuse occurring prior to age 6-8, a critical developmental period before a unified, cohesive sense of self is neurodevelopmentally consolidated. In the face of overwhelming trauma, the child utilizes profound dissociation as an extreme psychological survival defense: by walling off traumatic memories into segregated identity states ('alters'), the child preserves functioning and attachment to abusive caregivers.\n\nIn DSM-5, the diagnostic criteria were updated to recognize cultural diversity. DSM-5 explicitly acknowledges that in many non-Western cultures, distinct personality states may be experienced and described as instances of 'possession' by spirits, deities, or external entities. Furthermore, DSM-5 permits the disruption in identity to be reported subjectively by the individual rather than requiring objective observation by third parties.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: Disruption of identity characterized by two or more distinct personality states, which may be described in some cultures as an experience of possession. The disruption in identity involves marked discontinuity in sense of self and sense of agency, accompanied by related alterations in affect, behavior, consciousness, memory, perception, cognition, and/or sensory-motor functioning. (Note: These signs and symptoms may be observed by others or reported by the individual).\n\nCriterion B: Recurrent gaps in the recall of everyday events, important personal information, and/or traumatic events that are inconsistent with ordinary forgetting. (Includes gaps in remote autobiographical memory, everyday lapses such as finding unremembered possessions, and blackouts).\n\nCriterion C: The symptoms cause clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning.\n\nCriterion D: The disturbance is not a normal part of a broadly accepted cultural or religious practice. (In children, symptoms are not better explained by imaginary playmates or other fantasy play).\n\nCriterion E: The symptoms are not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., blackouts during alcohol intoxication) or another medical condition (e.g., complex partial seizures).",
        'specifiers' => "DID is coded as a standalone disorder. Clinicians document whether the presentation is overt (readily observable distinct alter switches) or covert (internal experiences of voices conversing, depersonalization, and memory gaps).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "DID vs. Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD): BPD involves identity diffusion, affective instability, and abandonment fears, but lacks distinct, segregated alter identities with discrete autobiographical memory amnesia boundaries. However, DID and BPD frequently co-occur.\n\nDID vs. Schizophrenia: In DID, auditory hallucinations are experienced internally as distinct alter voices conversing or arguing inside the head, and reality testing remains intact outside alter transitions; in Schizophrenia, hallucinations are experienced externally and paired with formal thought disorder and bizarre delusions.\n\nDID vs. Malingering / Factitious Disorder: Malingerers typically overemphasize dramatic, Hollywood-style symptoms, flaunt memory loss, and claim alters committed crimes to escape legal culpability; true DID patients feel intense shame and actively conceal their dissociative symptoms.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 32-year-old woman evaluated for severe memory gaps. She frequently finds unfamiliar clothing in her closet, discovers store receipts for items she has no memory of purchasing, and is greeted by strangers who address her by a different name.\n\nClinical evaluation reveals an extensive history of severe early childhood sexual abuse. Under clinical hypnosis and interview, two distinct identity states emerge: an assertive, protective alter with distinct speech patterns and posture, and a terrified child alter. The patient experiences complete amnesia for the actions of these alter states. The clinical formulation confirms Dissociative Identity Disorder.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: DID requires >= 2 distinct personality states (or experience of possession) AND recurrent gaps in everyday recall (dissociative amnesia) inconsistent with normal forgetting. Etiology is overwhelmingly linked to severe early childhood trauma before age 6-8. Cultural spirit possession can satisfy Criterion A if involuntary, distressing, and outside accepted religious practice. Dissociative auditory voices are experienced internally, distinguishing them from psychotic external hallucinations. True DID patients actively hide their symptoms due to shame.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Two or More Distinct Identities', 'icon' => '👥', 'definition' => 'Requires at least 2 distinct personality states with marked discontinuity in sense of self and agency.'],
            ['title' => 'Recurrent Dissociative Amnesia', 'icon' => '🕳️', 'definition' => 'Memory gaps for everyday events, personal information, or trauma that exceed ordinary forgetting.'],
            ['title' => 'Severe Early Childhood Trauma Etiology', 'icon' => '⛓️', 'definition' => 'Driven by extreme, inescapable childhood abuse interrupting the developmental consolidation of a unified self.'],
            ['title' => 'Cultural Spirit Possession Inclusion', 'icon' => '🌐', 'definition' => 'DSM-5 recognizes that alter states may manifest as possession experiences in specific cultural contexts.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Alters (Alternate Identity States)', 'definition' => 'Distinct identity states within an individual with DID, each with its own pattern of perceiving, relating, and thinking about self and environment.'],
            ['term' => 'Co-Consciousness', 'definition' => 'A therapeutic state wherein two or more identity states are simultaneously aware of ongoing experiences and internal communication.'],
            ['term' => 'Identity Fragmentation', 'definition' => 'The developmental failure to integrate separate behavioral, emotional, and cognitive self-states into a single unified identity.'],
            ['term' => 'Time Loss (Blackouts)', 'definition' => 'Subjective periods of missing time wherein an individual has no conscious recollection of actions executed while an alter was in executive control.']
        ]
    ],
    7 => [
        'title' => 'Dissociative Amnesia: Localized, Selective, and Generalized Memory Gaps',
        'slug' => 'dissociative-amnesia-memory-patterns',
        'description' => 'The three core memory amnesia patterns (localized, selective, generalized), trauma etiology, and organic neurological exclusions.',
        'overview' => "Dissociative Amnesia is characterized by an inability to recall important personal, autobiographical information—usually of a traumatic or stressful nature—that is inconsistent with ordinary forgetfulness. Unlike organic amnesias caused by neurodegenerative disease or physical trauma, Dissociative Amnesia is fundamentally functional and reversible: the encoded memories remain stored in the brain, but active retrieval is blocked by intense dissociative defense mechanisms.\n\nIn clinical practice, Dissociative Amnesia presents across three primary patterns: (1) Localized amnesia, the most common form, wherein the individual fails to recall all events that occurred during a circumscribed period of time (e.g., complete amnesia for the hours of a traumatic car crash); (2) Selective amnesia, wherein the individual can recall some, but not all, of the events during a circumscribed traumatic period (e.g., remembering entering a combat zone but forgetting the moment of an explosion); and (3) Generalized amnesia, a rare, profound form involving a complete loss of memory for one's entire life history and personal identity.\n\nIndividuals with Dissociative Amnesia are frequently unaware of their memory gaps until their amnesia is brought to their attention by others or when missing information is directly needed. Furthermore, organic neurological conditions—such as transient global amnesia, concussive closed-head trauma, or substance-induced blackouts—must be systematically excluded through neuroimaging and cognitive testing.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: An inability to recall important autobiographical information, usually of a traumatic or stressful nature, that is inconsistent with ordinary forgetting.\n(Note: Dissociative amnesia most often consists of localized or selective amnesia for a specific event or events; or generalized amnesia for identity and life history).\n\nCriterion B: The symptoms cause clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning.\n\nCriterion C: The disturbance is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., alcohol or other drug abuse, a medication) or a neurological or other medical condition (e.g., partial complex seizures, transient global amnesia, sequelae of closed head injury/traumatic brain injury, other neurological condition).\n\nCriterion D: The disturbance is not better explained by Dissociative Identity Disorder, PTSD, Acute Stress Disorder, Somatic Symptom Disorder, or Major or Mild Neurocognitive Disorder.",
        'specifiers' => "Mandatory Specifier: 'With dissociative fugue' (assigned if the amnesia is accompanied by purposeful travel or bewildered wandering that is associated with amnesia for identity or other important autobiographical information).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Dissociative Amnesia vs. Organic Neurological Amnesia (TBI / Alzheimer's): In organic amnesia, anterograde memory encoding is impaired (cannot learn new information) and procedural memory is preserved; in Dissociative Amnesia, anterograde memory is completely normal (can learn new tasks effortlessly), but retrograde autobiographical recall for specific traumatic windows is blocked.\n\nDissociative Amnesia vs. Substance-Induced Blackout: Alcohol blackouts involve acute pharmacological failure of hippocampal memory consolidation during severe intoxication; Dissociative Amnesia occurs during a clear sensorium.\n\nDissociative Amnesia vs. Dissociative Identity Disorder: If the amnesia occurs alongside distinct personality states (alters), DID is diagnosed, NOT Dissociative Amnesia.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 30-year-old soldier who survived an improvised explosive device (IED) attack in which their vehicle was destroyed. Neurological evaluation and MRI brain imaging reveal no structural injury or concussion.\n\nHowever, the soldier has zero memory for the four hours surrounding the explosion, unable to recall the blast, the evacuation, or the medical triage (localized amnesia). The soldier's memory for all past life events and their ability to learn new military orders is completely normal. The clinical formulation confirms Dissociative Amnesia, Localized type.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Dissociative Amnesia involves inability to recall autobiographical information, usually traumatic, inconsistent with normal forgetting. The 3 patterns are: Localized (most common; entire circumscribed period lost), Selective (some events remembered, others lost), and Generalized (rare; entire life history lost). Anterograde memory (learning new info) remains intact, distinguishing it from organic dementia. The Fugue specifier applies if purposeful travel or bewildered wandering occurs.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Three Amnestic Patterns', 'icon' => '🧩', 'definition' => 'Localized (entire time period lost), Selective (partial recall of period), and Generalized (entire life history lost).'],
            ['title' => 'Intact Anterograde Encoding', 'icon' => '🧠', 'definition' => 'The individual learns new facts and skills normally, distinguishing functional amnesia from organic hippocampal damage.'],
            ['title' => 'Reversible Functional Retrieval Failure', 'icon' => '🔓', 'definition' => 'Memories are biologically stored but psychogenically blocked from conscious retrieval, allowing eventual recovery.'],
            ['title' => 'The Dissociative Fugue Specifier', 'icon' => '🧳', 'definition' => 'Assigned when amnesia is accompanied by purposeful travel or bewildered wandering away from home.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Localized Amnesia', 'definition' => 'A failure to recall all events that occurred during a specific, circumscribed period of time, the most common form of dissociative amnesia.'],
            ['term' => 'Selective Amnesia', 'definition' => 'The capacity to recall some, but not all, of the events occurring during a circumscribed traumatic period.'],
            ['term' => 'Generalized Amnesia', 'definition' => 'A rare, profound loss of memory for one\'s entire life history, identity, and personal biographical facts.'],
            ['term' => 'Transient Global Amnesia (TGA)', 'definition' => 'An organic neurological syndrome characterized by sudden, temporary loss of short-term memory with repetitive questioning, resolving in 24 hours.']
        ]
    ],
    8 => [
        'title' => 'Dissociative Fugue Specifier: Purposeful Travel & Identity Confusion',
        'slug' => 'dissociative-fugue-specifier-travel',
        'description' => 'The fugue specifier criteria, purposeful travel vs. bewildered wandering, assumption of new identities, and abrupt resolution.',
        'overview' => "Dissociative Fugue represents an extraordinary, dramatic clinical specifier within Dissociative Amnesia. In DSM-IV, Dissociative Fugue was classified as a standalone psychiatric disorder; however, in DSM-5, extensive clinical evidence confirmed that fugue episodes are phenomenologically indistinguishable from Dissociative Amnesia with generalized or severe memory loss. Consequently, DSM-5 reclassified Dissociative Fugue as an explicit course specifier for Dissociative Amnesia ('Dissociative Amnesia, With dissociative fugue').\n\nThe hallmark of Dissociative Fugue is amnesia for autobiographical identity accompanied by purposeful travel or bewildered wandering away from home, workplace, or familiar surroundings. While in a fugue state, individuals may travel hundreds or thousands of miles, during which their outward behavior may appear entirely purposeful, polite, and unexceptional to observers.\n\nIn severe cases, an individual in a fugue state may assume a completely new identity, establish a new residence, obtain employment, and interact socially without exhibiting overt signs of psychopathology. Fugue episodes are almost universally precipitated by intolerable psychosocial stressors (such as combat, financial ruin, marital crisis, or severe emotional trauma). The termination of a fugue episode is characteristically abrupt: the individual suddenly 'awakens' to their original identity, frequently experiencing complete amnesia for the events that transpired during the fugue state itself.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Specifier Formulation under DSM-5:\n\n'With dissociative fugue': Applied when Dissociative Amnesia is characterized by apparently purposeful travel or bewildered wandering that is associated with amnesia for identity or for other important autobiographical information.\n\nClinical Parameters for Verification:\n1. Purposeful travel (e.g., buying bus tickets, driving cross-country, checking into hotels) or bewildered wandering.\n2. Inability to recall one's previous personal identity or life history.\n3. Possible assumption of a new identity (partial or complete).\n4. The travel and amnesia must not occur exclusively during Dissociative Identity Disorder.\n5. The disturbance must not be attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., alcohol intoxication blackouts) or a general medical condition (e.g., complex partial seizures, post-ictal wandering, transient global amnesia).",
        'specifiers' => "Dissociative Fugue is coded as a specifier under Dissociative Amnesia: e.g., 'Dissociative Amnesia, With dissociative fugue'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Dissociative Fugue vs. Complex Partial Seizures: Seizure automatisms involve brief, confused, purposeless wandering lasting minutes, followed by post-ictal confusion; fugue involves purposeful, complex travel lasting days, weeks, or months.\n\nDissociative Fugue vs. Malingering: Malingering must be vigorously evaluated when individuals claim amnesic fugue while facing criminal prosecution, military desertion charges, or financial bankruptcy; malingerers often exaggerate confusion and inconsistency on standardized testing.\n\nDissociative Fugue vs. Bipolar Mania: Manic travel is driven by grandiosity, pressured energy, and decreased sleep need, without loss of personal identity.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 45-year-old bank executive facing impending corporate bankruptcy and fraud charges. Following a heated legal meeting, the executive vanished. Three weeks later, the executive was discovered working as a short-order cook 1,200 miles away in a different state under an assumed name.\n\nWhen questioned by local police, the individual suddenly 'awakened', expressed profound confusion about where they were, remembered their true name and family, but had complete amnesia for the three weeks of travel and diner employment. Detailed neurological workup is negative. The purposeful travel and identity amnesia confirm Dissociative Amnesia, With Dissociative Fugue.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: In DSM-5, Dissociative Fugue is a SPECIFIER for Dissociative Amnesia, NOT a standalone disorder. Defined by purposeful travel or bewildered wandering associated with amnesia for identity. Individuals may assume a new identity. Episodes resolve abruptly, followed by amnesia for the fugue period. Rule out malingering (legal/financial escape) and complex partial seizures. Triggered by overwhelming psychosocial trauma or crisis.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'DSM-5 Specifier Status', 'icon' => '🏷️', 'definition' => 'Reclassified from a standalone DSM-IV disorder into a specific course specifier under Dissociative Amnesia.'],
            ['title' => 'Purposeful Travel Benchmark', 'icon' => '🧳', 'definition' => 'Requires purposeful travel (e.g., purchasing tickets, navigating transit) or bewildered wandering away from home.'],
            ['title' => 'Assumption of New Identity', 'icon' => '🎭', 'definition' => 'Severe fugue states may involve adopting a new name, employment, and persona that appear outwardly normal.'],
            ['title' => 'Abrupt Resolution Dynamics', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'Fugue states typically terminate suddenly, followed by complete amnesia for events that occurred during the fugue.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Fugue State', 'definition' => 'A dissociative state characterized by flight from familiar surroundings, loss of personal identity, and the execution of purposeful travel.'],
            ['term' => 'Bewildered Wandering', 'definition' => 'Aimless, disoriented ambulation away from home without clear destination planning, observed in less organized fugue presentations.'],
            ['term' => 'Post-Fugue Amnesia', 'definition' => 'Amnesia covering the period of the fugue itself that emerges immediately upon the spontaneous return of original identity.'],
            ['term' => 'Psychogenic Flight', 'definition' => 'The psychological mechanism of physically escaping an intolerable life trauma while simultaneously blocking conscious memory of the self.']
        ]
    ],
    9 => [
        'title' => 'Differential Diagnosis: PTSD vs. Acute Stress vs. Adjustment vs. Panic vs. Borderline Personality',
        'slug' => 'differential-diagnosis-trauma-dissociative-disorders',
        'description' => 'Systematic differential algorithm: trauma qualifying thresholds, temporal milestones, and boundary analysis.',
        'overview' => "The differential diagnosis of Trauma-, Stressor-Related and Dissociative Disorders demands the systematic application of etiological gates and temporal milestones. Because symptoms of hyperarousal, emotional numbness, dissociation, and behavioral avoidance overlap extensively across psychiatric conditions, arriving at the correct diagnostic classification carries direct implications for trauma-focused psychotherapy and legal documentation.\n\nThe initial branch point requires determining whether the precipitating stressor meets DSM-5 Criterion A trauma (actual or threatened death, serious injury, sexual violence). If YES, the diagnosis is governed by duration: symptoms lasting 3 days to 1 month -> Acute Stress Disorder; symptoms lasting > 1 month -> PTSD. If the stressor does NOT meet Criterion A (e.g., divorce, business failure) -> Adjustment Disorder.\n\nFurthermore, clinicians must distinguish trauma-induced hyperarousal from Panic Disorder (where attacks are unexpected and unprompted by trauma memories) and Borderline Personality Disorder (where identity disturbance is characterological and chronic, rather than fragmented into discrete dissociative alter states). This module provides a definitive decision tree to resolve complex trauma and dissociative presentations.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Systematic Differential Algorithm:\n\n1. Step 1: Stressor Qualification (Criterion A):\n   • Does the event involve actual/threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence? If YES -> Trauma Spectrum (PTSD/ASD). If NO -> Adjustment Disorder.\n\n2. Step 2: Temporal Duration Gate (If Criterion A is Met):\n   • 3 days to 1 month -> Acute Stress Disorder.\n   • > 1 month -> Posttraumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD).\n\n3. Step 3: Identity & Memory Evaluation:\n   • Presence of 2+ distinct personality states with amnesia -> Dissociative Identity Disorder (DID).\n   • Autobiographical memory gaps without alters -> Dissociative Amnesia.\n   • Memory gaps accompanied by purposeful travel -> Dissociative Amnesia, With Dissociative Fugue.\n\n4. Step 4: Rule Out Mimics (Panic Disorder, BPD, Malingering).",
        'specifiers' => "Diagnostic formulations record primary trauma/dissociative diagnoses, course specifiers ('With dissociative symptoms', 'With delayed expression', 'With dissociative fugue'), and comorbidity.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "PTSD with Dissociative Symptoms vs. DID: In PTSD with dissociative symptoms, depersonalization/derealization occurs without distinct personality states (alters); in DID, distinct alter states with separate autobiographical memories are present.\n\nAdjustment Disorder vs. PTSD: Adjustment disorder involves non-Criterion A stressors (e.g., divorce, financial loss) and must resolve within 6 months of stressor termination; PTSD requires Criterion A trauma and features intrusion/avoidance clusters.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 30-year-old nurse who experienced a severe physical assault at work two weeks ago. The nurse has nightmares, avoids the hospital, feels emotionally numb, is hypervigilant, and startles easily (10 symptoms across 4 clusters).\n\nEvaluating the timeline: The stressor is Criterion A (physical assault/serious injury). Symptoms have persisted for 14 days (within the 3-day to 1-month window). The accurate diagnosis is Acute Stress Disorder, NOT PTSD. If symptoms continue beyond 30 days, the diagnosis will officially convert to PTSD.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: The differential hierarchy: Criterion A trauma + 3 days to 1 month = Acute Stress Disorder. Criterion A trauma + > 1 month = PTSD. Non-Criterion A stressor (divorce, job loss) = Adjustment Disorder. 2+ distinct personality states with memory gaps = Dissociative Identity Disorder. Reversible autobiographical memory loss = Dissociative Amnesia. Purposeful travel with amnesia = Dissociative Fugue specifier.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The Criterion A Gateway Test', 'icon' => '🚪', 'definition' => 'Differentiating trauma disorders (PTSD/ASD) from stressor disorders (Adjustment) based on death/injury threat.'],
            ['title' => 'Temporal Boundary Demarcation', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Strict 30-day boundary separating Acute Stress Disorder (<1 month) from chronic PTSD (>1 month).'],
            ['title' => 'Alter Identity vs. Depersonalization', 'icon' => '👥', 'definition' => 'Distinguishing discrete alter states in DID from transient feelings of detachment in PTSD with dissociative symptoms.'],
            ['title' => 'Malingering Differentiation Protocol', 'icon' => '🔍', 'definition' => 'Systematic evaluation of external legal, financial, or military incentives in patients claiming amnesic fugue.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Criterion A Gateway', 'definition' => 'The initial threshold test determining whether an event qualifies as trauma involving death, serious injury, or sexual violence.'],
            ['term' => 'Diagnostic Threshold Window', 'definition' => 'The specific duration boundaries (3 days, 1 month, 6 months) that govern classification across the trauma spectrum.'],
            ['term' => 'Identity Diffusion', 'definition' => 'A poorly integrated sense of self and values seen in Borderline Personality Disorder, distinct from DID alter fragmentation.'],
            ['term' => 'Delayed Expression Window', 'definition' => 'The onset of full PTSD diagnostic criteria at least 6 months after the traumatic exposure.']
        ]
    ],
    10 => [
        'title' => 'Chapter Mastery & Board-Exam Synthesis',
        'slug' => 'chapter-mastery-board-exam-synthesis-trauma-dissociative',
        'description' => 'Comprehensive synthesis of Trauma-, Stressor-Related and Dissociative Disorders, diagnostic criteria, and high-yield board pearls.',
        'overview' => "This capstone unit delivers an advanced synthesis of Trauma-, Stressor-Related and Dissociative Disorders under DSM-5 criteria, consolidating core diagnostic formulations, duration thresholds, and high-yield board pearls for professional licensure examinations. Trauma and dissociative pathology represents heavily tested content across clinical examinations, demanding exact command over diagnostic criteria, specifiers, and differential boundaries.\n\nMastery requires commanding the clinical distinctions separating Posttraumatic Stress Disorder, Acute Stress Disorder, Adjustment Disorders, Dissociative Identity Disorder, and Dissociative Amnesia. Furthermore, candidates must navigate DSM-5 structural refinements, including the creation of the standalone Trauma chapter, the elimination of Criterion A2, the preschool developmental PTSD criteria, the reclassification of Dissociative Fugue as a specifier, and the Dissociative Subtype of PTSD.\n\nBy synthesizing clinical vignettes, diagnostic decision matrices, and evidence-based neurobiological mechanisms, this unit prepares students and clinicians for board examination excellence and compassionate clinical practice.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Mastery Summary of Core Trauma and Dissociative Rules:\n\n1. PTSD: Criterion A trauma exposure; 4 clusters: Intrusion (>=1), Avoidance (>=1), Cognition/Mood (>=2), Arousal (>=2); duration > 1 month. Specifiers: With dissociative symptoms, With delayed expression (>=6 mos).\n\n2. PTSD in Children <=6 Years: Developmentally adapted criteria; traumatic play reenactment; nightmares without clear content; combined Avoidance/Cognition cluster (>=1); Arousal (>=2); duration > 1 month.\n\n3. Acute Stress Disorder: Criterion A trauma; 9 of 14 symptoms across 5 categories; duration 3 days to 1 month. Converts to PTSD at day 31.\n\n4. Adjustment Disorders: Emotional/behavioral symptoms within 3 months of non-Criterion A stressor; out of proportion distress; resolves within 6 months of stressor termination; 6 subtypes; subthreshold for MDD.\n\n5. Dissociative Identity Disorder: >= 2 distinct personality states (or possession) + recurrent dissociative amnesia; early childhood trauma etiology.\n\n6. Dissociative Amnesia: Inability to recall autobiographical info (localized, selective, generalized); 'With dissociative fugue' specifier (purposeful travel / wandering).",
        'specifiers' => "Review of essential specifiers: 'With dissociative symptoms' in PTSD; 'With delayed expression' in PTSD; 'With dissociative fugue' in Dissociative Amnesia; 6 clinical subtypes in Adjustment Disorders.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Key Board Differential Pearls:\n• Symptoms lasting 2 weeks after violent assault -> Acute Stress Disorder (3 days to 1 month).\n• Symptoms lasting 6 weeks after violent assault -> PTSD (> 1 month).\n• Symptoms of depression and tearfulness 2 months after divorce -> Adjustment Disorder, With depressed mood (non-Criterion A).\n• Intrusive memories in a 4-year-old manifest as -> Traumatic play reenactment.\n• Two distinct personalities with everyday memory blackouts -> Dissociative Identity Disorder.\n• Executive found working across the country with amnesia for past identity -> Dissociative Amnesia, With Dissociative Fugue.\n• Electronic media exposure does NOT qualify for Criterion A unless work-related.",
        'clinical_example' => "A comprehensive clinical review scenario: A board examination vignette describes a 29-year-old firefighter who presents with severe nightmares, hypervigilance, and emotional numbness that began seven months after responding to a fatal building collapse. When exposed to sirens, the firefighter feels detached from their body, observing themselves from above (depersonalization).\n\nBoard Exam Question Formulation: What is the diagnosis and specifiers? The candidate identifies that the work-related fatal exposure satisfies Criterion A, symptoms meet all 4 clusters for >1 month, the emergence at 7 months satisfies 'With delayed expression' (>=6 months), and depersonalization satisfies 'With dissociative symptoms', confirming Posttraumatic Stress Disorder, With dissociative symptoms, With delayed expression.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board pearls summary: PTSD = > 1 month across 4 clusters. Acute Stress Disorder = 3 days to 1 month (9 of 14 symptoms). Adjustment Disorder = within 3 months of stressor, resolves within 6 months of termination. Preschool PTSD = <=6 years, play reenactment. DID = 2+ personality states + amnesia. Dissociative Fugue is a specifier for Dissociative Amnesia. Media exposure does NOT count unless work-related. DSM-5 eliminated Criterion A2.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Trauma Diagnostic Decision Matrix', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Comprehensive command of duration thresholds, symptom counts, and severity rules across all trauma/dissociative disorders.'],
            ['title' => 'The 30-Day ASD-to-PTSD Line', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Exact command of the temporal demarcation separating acute trauma (<1 month) from chronic PTSD (>1 month).'],
            ['title' => 'Preschool Developmental Adaptations', 'icon' => '🧸', 'definition' => 'Recognizing play reenactment, content-free nightmares, and merged clusters in children aged 6 and younger.'],
            ['title' => 'The Dissociative Spectrum Continuum', 'icon' => '🌫️', 'definition' => 'Understanding the progression from localized amnesia to fugue travel to complex Dissociative Identity Disorder.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Trauma Spectrum', 'definition' => 'The clinical continuum of conditions triggered by exposure to catastrophic events, ranging from acute stress reactions to chronic dissociative pathology.'],
            ['term' => 'Delayed Expression', 'definition' => 'A clinical specifier in PTSD applied when full diagnostic criteria are not satisfied until at least six months following trauma exposure.'],
            ['term' => 'Psychogenic Amnesia', 'definition' => 'Reversible memory loss for personal biographical events driven by psychological trauma rather than organic neurological damage.'],
            ['term' => 'Trauma-Focused CBT', 'definition' => 'The gold-standard psychological therapy for PTSD incorporating psychoeducation, cognitive restructuring, and graduated trauma narrative exposure.']
        ]
    ]
];
